\description{
Selects samples of each size in \code{n_grid}, routes each one from the depot
(or bases), prices it with the cost model, and fits the expected cost as
\verb{G(n) = c0 + a n + b sqrt(n)}. This is the cost function of Hansen, Hurwitz
and Madow (1953, Vol. I, Chapter 6, Section 12), \verb{C0 sqrt(m) + C1 m}, with \code{b} in
the role of their travel constant \code{C0} and \code{a} of the cost per primary unit
\code{C1}. They derived \code{C0} from points on a regular grid and called it a rough
approximation; here it is measured on the frame, with its roads (when the
matrix comes from OSRM), its depots and its route limits. The travel of a
tour through \code{n} scattered points grows like \code{sqrt(n)} (Beardwood, Halton
and Hammersley 1959), and fixed costs per visit and per route add the
linear term.
}
\details{
The fit is constrained to be increasing and concave (\code{a >= 0}, \code{b >= 0}),
so the marginal cost \verb{G'(n) = a + b / (2 sqrt(n))} is positive and falls
with \code{n}. The marginal cost is the one the classical allocation formulas
need (see \code{\link[=two_stage_design]{two_stage_design()}}).
